<!--
    Type campaign, item `charptr`: a pointer the program only ever uses on
    characters is declared as an integer or as `void *`, although the fact is
    already on the table.  coreutils `[` -O0 shows both halves:

        char *sub_50ba(unsigned long a0, void *a1)

    where `a1` is handed straight to `fputs_unlocked`, whose libc signature
    declares `char *` there.

    Root cause (P5, type propagation).  `TypeOpCall::getInputLocal`
    (typeop.cc:689) states the callee's declared parameter type about the
    Varnode that IS the call's operand.  Every hop between the parameter and
    that operand - an -O0 spill through the frame, a MULTIEQUAL, a `p + k` -
    is a hop `propagateTypeEdge` will not carry a pointer back over
    (`TypeOpIntAdd::propagateType`, typeop.cc:1217, is the same refusal
    `ptrfromuse` documents).  So the declaration reaches the argument and stops
    there.

    Stage / sub-stage: P5 "type-propagation".  The P0 assertion
    `option charptr off|libc|uses` decides which evidence may commit a
    pointer-width parameter or stack local to `char *`.

    The fixture is the same shape on SysV x86-64, plus three controls:

        spillcall:  add rdi,1; call slen     where `char *slen(char *)`
                    the witness for `libc` - the declaration is one INT_ADD
                    away from the parameter, which is the hop propagation
                    cannot cross
        derefbyte:  movzx eax, byte [rdi+1]
                    the witness for `uses` - the only dereference through the
                    parameter is one byte wide.  `ptrfromuse` already makes it
                    `void *`; this is what decides what it points AT
        scaled:     shl rdi,2; call slen
                    a control - RDI is SHIFTED before it reaches the declared
                    `char *`, which no pointer survives, so the rule declines
                    in every pass
        mixedwidth: movzx eax, byte [rdi+1]; add eax,[rdi+8]
                    a control - the same parameter is also read four bytes
                    wide, so the byte read does not establish the element type
                    and the candidate is withdrawn
        otherproto: add rdi,1; call ilen     where `int8 ilen(int8 *)`
                    a control - the callee declares a pointer at something
                    else, which contradicts a character pointer rather than
                    supporting one

    Pass 1 (`option charptr off`) reproduces the bug; pass 2 (`libc`) moves the
    call-site witness only; pass 3 (`uses`) moves the dereference witness too.
    All three controls are identical in all three passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000">

</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="spillcall"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100020" name="scaled"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100040" name="derefbyte"/>
